00:10:10.019 --> 00:10:11.166
- [Voiceover] George asks,

00:10:11.166 --> 00:10:12.935
"I'm curious to see where
grandmavee is headed.

00:10:12.935 --> 00:10:14.790
"Seems to be getting 
a lot of traction.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.471
"Are you going to 
make it a thing?"

00:10:16.471 --> 00:10:18.128
- For all of you that 
missed Grandma Vee

00:10:18.128 --> 00:10:21.658
over I guess Wednesday
and Thursday of last week.

00:10:21.658 --> 00:10:24.371
There was a filter on
Snapchat, I went into character

00:10:24.371 --> 00:10:26.579
called Grandma Vee, grannyvee.

00:10:26.579 --> 00:10:29.637
I converted it to grannyvee
because it's just better.

00:10:29.637 --> 00:10:31.304
And you know, she was
just, basically it was just

00:10:31.304 --> 00:10:33.725
really funny about it, she
was just basically saying

00:10:33.725 --> 00:10:36.233
the same things I say 
except it's funny.

00:10:36.233 --> 00:10:38.016
I think of myself 
as an old soul,

00:10:38.016 --> 00:10:39.037
and it just makes more sense.

00:10:39.037 --> 00:10:40.666
Basically, she, I just made videos

00:10:40.666 --> 00:10:42.576
on Snapchat where 
she said, in my day,

00:10:42.576 --> 00:10:44.287
and then basically said
the things I believe in.

00:10:44.287 --> 00:10:47.485
And it was a lot of fun,
and boy did it catch fire.

00:10:47.485 --> 00:10:49.002
People really 
passionate about it.

00:10:49.002 --> 00:10:53.043
Unfortunate the granny 
filter is gone now,

00:10:53.043 --> 00:10:55.303
and I dunno if they're
bringing it back so.

00:10:55.303 --> 00:10:57.014
I don't know where it's going.

00:10:57.014 --> 00:10:58.477
I guess we could design it.

00:10:58.477 --> 00:10:59.922
I guess Andrew could just draw it

00:10:59.922 --> 00:11:01.455
and we could put it 
over my head at times.

00:11:01.455 --> 00:11:03.013
I do think I should 
do a grannyvee episode

00:11:03.013 --> 00:11:04.599
of The AskGrannyVee Show.

00:11:04.599 --> 00:11:06.879
I'm super willing to do it if
Andrew is willing to draw it

00:11:06.879 --> 00:11:09.290
and impose it, you guys
can figure it out, team.

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:11.067
I will do a 
whole episode, in costume.

00:11:11.067 --> 00:11:13.177
One little fun fact, Staphon,
you forget these edits

00:11:13.177 --> 00:11:15.671
sometimes, even still,
so do not miss this one.

00:11:15.671 --> 00:11:17.991
This is not the first time
I went into character.

00:11:17.991 --> 00:11:20.458
Way back in the day,
during Wine Library TV,

00:11:20.458 --> 00:11:23.844
there was a character
called Sir Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:11:23.844 --> 00:11:27.461
which was the classic,
more serious wine reviewer.

00:11:27.461 --> 00:11:29.481
Staphon, give them 
30 seconds right now.

00:11:29.481 --> 00:11:30.941
- The key to everything is this.

00:11:30.941 --> 00:11:33.870
Gary is in Hungary, and
it is now the Friday show.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.670
Now obviously he could
not do this on Monday

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.907
because it was raining,
and it was a Monday.

00:11:38.907 --> 00:11:41.452
And how could you possibly
do a Friday show on Friday.

00:11:41.452 --> 00:11:43.666
So I threw the couch out the window,

00:11:43.666 --> 00:11:46.603
and I jumped in and I've
taken over the show today.

00:11:46.603 --> 00:11:50.353
He's not here, call it
a Coup d'etat for you.

00:11:51.215 --> 00:11:53.300
- So I've been known 
to do this before,

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.753
and it has inspired me to
consider going into character

00:11:56.753 --> 00:11:58.487
on Snapchat filters more often

00:11:58.487 --> 00:11:59.620
'cause I think 
it's interesting content,

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:00.703
I think I can pull it off,

00:12:00.703 --> 00:12:02.352
and I think it's a blue
print for a lot of you.

00:12:02.352 --> 00:12:03.666
I think a lot of you 
that are struggling

00:12:03.666 --> 00:12:07.087
that are comedians, or improv
actors, that have that DNA

00:12:07.087 --> 00:12:09.542
that are figuring out
what to do on Snapchat.

00:12:09.542 --> 00:12:11.582
I do think the filters
and all these characters

00:12:11.582 --> 00:12:15.836
is an incredible 
storytelling opportunity.

00:12:15.836 --> 00:12:17.510
- [Voiceover] Brandon
asks, "Why are the reviews

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:21.176
"for VaynerMedia on 
GlassDoor so bad?"

00:12:21.176 --> 00:12:23.799
- So this one's tough for me.

00:12:23.799 --> 00:12:26.231
There's a lot of reasons why

00:12:26.231 --> 00:12:29.244
our reviews on 
GlassDoor for VaynerMedia

00:12:29.244 --> 00:12:30.925
are not as good 
as they should be

00:12:30.925 --> 00:12:32.566
or the reality of our culture.

00:12:32.566 --> 00:12:34.671
I think, first of all, 
I think it has a lot to do

00:12:34.671 --> 00:12:35.871
with many different things.

00:12:35.871 --> 00:12:37.489
First of all, 
anonymous websites, right?

00:12:37.489 --> 00:12:40.181
So you look at Secret and
Whisper and Anonymous,

00:12:40.181 --> 00:12:43.598
you're gonna get people that
are willing to go to extremes

00:12:43.598 --> 00:12:46.996
when nobody can figure
out who that person is.

00:12:46.996 --> 00:12:48.596
So anonymous leads to it.

00:12:48.596 --> 00:12:53.050
Two, we've gone from 30 to
650 people in four years.

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:54.454
There's a lot of pain in that,

00:12:54.454 --> 00:12:58.829
and a lot of people that
are employees struggle

00:12:58.829 --> 00:13:00.964
with the constant change and
the different decision making.

00:13:00.964 --> 00:13:02.737
I move very quickly 
and a lot of times

00:13:02.737 --> 00:13:04.118
I don't do the greatest job

00:13:04.118 --> 00:13:06.300
explaining my 
actions to everybody,

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:08.217
and I think that creates, 
you know,

00:13:08.217 --> 00:13:09.418
I think that a lot of movement,

00:13:09.418 --> 00:13:11.439
and a lot of structural 
changes creates

00:13:11.439 --> 00:13:14.269
a lot of angst for people
that are more comfortable

00:13:14.269 --> 00:13:15.765
and it's easy for 
the entrepreneur,

00:13:15.765 --> 00:13:17.892
it's harder for the employees.

00:13:17.892 --> 00:13:20.973
Three, I think cynicism is
unfortunately quite powerful,

00:13:20.973 --> 00:13:24.660
so I think that that's the case.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:28.273
I think some of the
early ones back in 2011

00:13:28.273 --> 00:13:30.247
are just, I think I did a bad job.

00:13:30.247 --> 00:13:32.663
I wasn't really the 
active CEO of Vayner,

00:13:32.663 --> 00:13:35.157
and I don't think we
micromanaged our youngest talent.

00:13:35.157 --> 00:13:38.437
So I feel good, I mean I feel
bad, but good about the ones

00:13:38.437 --> 00:13:41.160
that are in 11, I think
the last 15 of them,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.674
and I read them all 
50 times a month

00:13:43.674 --> 00:13:45.882
are coming from employees
that I don't think

00:13:45.882 --> 00:13:47.429
we saw the world the same way.

00:13:47.429 --> 00:13:49.047
I mean, I don't know 
what people expect

00:13:49.047 --> 00:13:51.527
from former employees, 
but if they're fired

00:13:51.527 --> 00:13:53.798
or if they quit, they
obviously don't feel great

00:13:53.798 --> 00:13:55.191
about the business, 
and that's okay.

00:13:55.191 --> 00:13:56.958
I mean, I think you 
go look at any,

00:13:56.958 --> 00:13:59.688
the is why I think 
Yelp and GlassDoor,

00:13:59.688 --> 00:14:01.871
and all these 
anonymous sites struggle,

00:14:01.871 --> 00:14:04.852
because what you don't have is,

00:14:04.852 --> 00:14:06.440
I'm not gonna do 
what most companies do,

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.294
which try to incentivize 
their employees

00:14:08.294 --> 00:14:11.515
to leave positive reviews to
offset the negative reviews.

00:14:11.515 --> 00:14:13.208
That's not gonna happen.

00:14:13.208 --> 00:14:15.147
I use them as feedback loops.

00:14:15.147 --> 00:14:16.195
Even though we try here.

00:14:16.195 --> 00:14:18.501
But these are people 
that are not happy

00:14:18.501 --> 00:14:19.703
with the way we're doing things.

00:14:19.703 --> 00:14:22.137
But I think the problem
is the silent majority.

00:14:22.137 --> 00:14:23.640
It's kind of like the Britain Exit.

00:14:23.640 --> 00:14:24.724
I have so many
friends who are like,

00:14:24.724 --> 00:14:26.046
I wish I voted.

00:14:26.046 --> 00:14:27.501
I mean, you had a 
chance but you didn't.

00:14:27.501 --> 00:14:29.480
I think its politics, right?

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.415
There's a lot of people
that don't talk about

00:14:31.415 --> 00:14:33.615
their views on, you hear it,

00:14:33.615 --> 00:14:37.159
you look on the internet,
you see the loud minorities

00:14:37.159 --> 00:14:39.241
dictate a narrative.

00:14:39.241 --> 00:14:41.509
And then the 
punchline is mainly,

00:14:41.509 --> 00:14:42.841
I think everything 
starts from the top,

00:14:42.841 --> 00:14:46.017
which is I actually like having
a bad review on GlassDoor,

00:14:46.017 --> 00:14:50.393
I think that any individual
that takes an anonymous website

00:14:50.393 --> 00:14:53.520
of former employees, 
and people say

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:54.837
they're current employees 
that are former,

00:14:54.837 --> 00:14:57.174
I had somebody who 
I did a nice thing for,

00:14:57.174 --> 00:14:59.614
call me and say look, I just
wanted you to know something,

00:14:59.614 --> 00:15:01.927
when I left a bad review, this
is somebody I did something

00:15:01.927 --> 00:15:03.592
nice for a year after 
they are fired.

00:15:03.592 --> 00:15:06.144
They called me 
because they felt guilty,

00:15:06.144 --> 00:15:08.056
and said I left a 
really nasty review

00:15:08.056 --> 00:15:09.274
because I felt that was unfair.

00:15:09.274 --> 00:15:11.046
I was super immature, 
and I also left it

00:15:11.046 --> 00:15:13.052
as a current employee 
because I thought

00:15:13.052 --> 00:15:14.584
that would hurt you more.

00:15:14.584 --> 00:15:16.425
So like, you know, 
I think that anybody

00:15:16.425 --> 00:15:19.909
who would stay 
out of a company,

00:15:19.909 --> 00:15:21.880
any company looking at,
Vayner what have you

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.290
because of GlassDoor or
any other anonymous site

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:26.581
as the proxy to what's
actually happening there,

00:15:26.581 --> 00:15:29.196
is exactly the kind of
people that I wanna keep out.

00:15:29.196 --> 00:15:31.472
It's the people playing
checkers versus chess,

00:15:31.472 --> 00:15:33.561
they're so, I don't
wanna say basic minded,

00:15:33.561 --> 00:15:35.230
because I don't 
wanna razz, but like,

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:37.010
I don't understand how
somebody wouldn't understand

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:38.892
that this is an 
anonymous site of people

00:15:38.892 --> 00:15:40.451
that are the least 
happy about something.

00:15:40.451 --> 00:15:41.954
So I think there's 
a lot of reasons

00:15:41.954 --> 00:15:43.941
we have a tough review there.

00:15:43.941 --> 00:15:44.872
They don't make me happy.

00:15:44.872 --> 00:15:47.926
I'm really upset when people
are unhappy with things,

00:15:47.926 --> 00:15:49.728
but I'll be honest with
you, when you're the CEO

00:15:49.728 --> 00:15:52.165
of a company that has gone
through 1000's of employees

00:15:52.165 --> 00:15:54.922
over the last four years, I've
has a lot more conversations

00:15:54.922 --> 00:15:56.885
one on one with somebody
saying that they're mad at me,

00:15:56.885 --> 00:15:59.234
or they don't like things and
they're saying it to my face,

00:15:59.234 --> 00:16:01.418
that hurts even kind of more
because I respect them so much

00:16:01.418 --> 00:16:02.749
for going that route.

00:16:02.749 --> 00:16:04.341
And I wanna fix it for them.

00:16:04.341 --> 00:16:05.774
You know, I'm in the
firefighting business.

00:16:05.774 --> 00:16:07.629
Everything is always hard.

00:16:07.629 --> 00:16:09.311
There's always problems.

00:16:09.311 --> 00:16:10.922
But I'm pretty confident

00:16:10.922 --> 00:16:13.534
about what's actually 
happening here.

00:16:13.534 --> 00:16:15.159
I think that shows 
in it's results.

00:16:15.159 --> 00:16:17.865
I think when you look at the
macro turnover rates here,

00:16:17.865 --> 00:16:19.913
voluntary turnover 
is what I look at,

00:16:19.913 --> 00:16:21.553
we're crushing the market.

00:16:21.553 --> 00:16:23.832
People aren't leaving
because they want, you know,

00:16:23.832 --> 00:16:25.073
'cause they don't like it here.

00:16:25.073 --> 00:16:27.530
And so, there's always gonna
be a percentage of people

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.066
that don't like certain things,

00:16:30.066 --> 00:16:32.355
and I don't think I'm perfect
or this company is perfect.

00:16:32.355 --> 00:16:35.001
Especially because we're always
making different changes.

00:16:35.001 --> 00:16:37.916
But I know that no CEO
organization has more intent

00:16:37.916 --> 00:16:40.089
to have people 
talk to us about it.

00:16:40.089 --> 00:16:41.858
Some people, 
I look at my own father,

00:16:41.858 --> 00:16:44.299
some people keep things inside
and will never share it,

00:16:44.299 --> 00:16:46.318
and so I can't fix 
what I don't know,

00:16:46.318 --> 00:16:47.631
and then when it 
doesn't work out,

00:16:47.631 --> 00:16:49.189
whether on their 
choice or our choice

00:16:49.189 --> 00:16:50.999
because they kept 
that poison in,

00:16:50.999 --> 00:16:53.687
a lot of those same
people can't communicate

00:16:53.687 --> 00:16:56.936
in real life but love
to go on anonymous site

00:16:56.936 --> 00:16:58.742
and get that poison out.

00:16:58.742 --> 00:17:00.841
And to be frank with you,

00:17:00.841 --> 00:17:03.830
I actually love GlassDoor,
for A helping me not allow

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.479
people to come in 
that have low EQ

00:17:05.479 --> 00:17:06.964
and are looking at 
a basic level,

00:17:06.964 --> 00:17:09.576
and B, I'm happy that
those people are able

00:17:09.576 --> 00:17:11.469
to get their poison of
what they feel about me

00:17:11.469 --> 00:17:13.252
or VaynerMedia out of their body

00:17:13.252 --> 00:17:15.560
and move on just like I
talked about in the beginning.

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:19.007
I hope it helps, 
and I hope with that out,

00:17:19.007 --> 00:17:21.607
they can take a step back,
look at the big picture

00:17:21.607 --> 00:17:24.119
and maybe reach out to me and
continue our relationship.

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:25.984
I'm very proud of my relationship

00:17:25.984 --> 00:17:28.521
with a lot of former employees
that left on bad terms.

00:17:28.521 --> 00:17:29.888
And I will continue 
to do that forever

00:17:29.888 --> 00:17:31.188
because I care about my legacy.

00:17:31.188 --> 00:17:33.799
Because I'm more selfish
about that than the money.

00:17:33.799 --> 00:17:34.632
Than things that nature.

00:17:34.632 --> 00:17:37.181
And by the way, you know,
as somebody who's very close

00:17:37.181 --> 00:17:40.151
to their business, 
I would say I'm 70% sure

00:17:40.151 --> 00:17:42.205
of the exact person
that leaves that review

00:17:42.205 --> 00:17:45.514
and there's a lot of, 
I'm never confused.

00:17:45.514 --> 00:17:46.732
You know, it just makes sense,

00:17:46.732 --> 00:17:49.387
like sometimes 
things don't go well.

00:17:49.387 --> 00:17:51.373
Inter-people relationships
and things of that nature

00:17:51.373 --> 00:17:54.613
and so it is what it is.

00:17:54.613 --> 00:17:57.608
It's a challenge 
because I care so much,

00:17:57.608 --> 00:17:59.729
but if you're watching
this and you're currently

00:17:59.729 --> 00:18:01.468
at Vayner, or you used 
to be at Vayner

00:18:01.468 --> 00:18:03.534
and you still have 
negativity in you,

00:18:03.534 --> 00:18:04.701
I'm super available.

00:18:04.701 --> 00:18:07.251
I'm Gary at VaynerMedia,
I've always been available.

00:18:07.251 --> 00:18:08.838
I'll always be available.

00:18:08.838 --> 00:18:11.238
And I think that to me
is much more interesting.

00:18:11.238 --> 00:18:13.396
You know, I'm not worried
about the perception.

00:18:13.396 --> 00:18:16.212
GlassDoor's 2.6 rating 
on VaynerMedia

00:18:16.212 --> 00:18:19.895
isn't stopping the 1000
applications a week we're getting

00:18:19.895 --> 00:18:21.914
or the growth of my company.

00:18:21.914 --> 00:18:26.131
I care way more about the 20
people that are upset with me

00:18:26.131 --> 00:18:28.686
or this, I'm here to fix it.

00:18:28.686 --> 00:18:30.977
Forever, if there's
actually something to fix,

00:18:30.977 --> 00:18:32.977
and even if there's not.

00:18:34.898 --> 00:18:36.089
- [Britt] Do you
have time for one more?

00:18:36.089 --> 00:18:37.132
- Yeah.

00:18:37.132 --> 00:18:38.306
I felt good about that answer.

00:18:39.936 --> 00:18:41.912
- [Voiceover] Tony says,
"Going to be deep here.

00:18:41.912 --> 00:18:44.401
"How do you deal with people
who take advantage of you?

00:18:44.401 --> 00:18:45.968
"You give, never take.

00:18:45.968 --> 00:18:47.569
"Does it get lonely?"

00:18:47.569 --> 00:18:50.147
- Yeah, I mean I think 
what I tend to do.

00:18:50.147 --> 00:18:51.991
I'm tying a lot of 
these things together.

00:18:51.991 --> 00:18:53.474
One of the reasons 
I don't try to manipulate

00:18:53.474 --> 00:18:55.723
GlassDoor like all my
friend's companies do

00:18:55.723 --> 00:18:59.127
is it helps you 
actually get the truth.

00:18:59.127 --> 00:19:01.492
I think one of the
things that's great about

00:19:01.492 --> 00:19:05.513
meritocracy, open ended,
not holding grudges

00:19:05.513 --> 00:19:06.877
is that you get to see truths.

00:19:06.877 --> 00:19:08.879
One of the things that I
think I've done quite well,

00:19:08.879 --> 00:19:10.746
and I highly recommend 
to a lot of you

00:19:10.746 --> 00:19:13.058
is not to manipulate situations.

00:19:13.058 --> 00:19:16.207
So by letting things
play out, you get hurt.

00:19:16.207 --> 00:19:17.716
But because I'm so 
emotionally strong,

00:19:17.716 --> 00:19:20.223
or I like to think I am, 
it gives me data.

00:19:20.223 --> 00:19:22.879
When people hurt me 
or take me for granted

00:19:22.879 --> 00:19:24.624
or don't see the bigger picture,

00:19:24.624 --> 00:19:26.159
I don't look at that 
as a negative,

00:19:26.159 --> 00:19:27.534
I look at it as a data point.

00:19:27.534 --> 00:19:29.546
It allows me to decide
what opportunities I want

00:19:29.546 --> 00:19:31.865
to give that person 
if I'm in control.

00:19:31.865 --> 00:19:33.148
Do I wanna do things 
with that person

00:19:33.148 --> 00:19:34.631
if I'm not in control.

00:19:34.631 --> 00:19:36.846
Do I give that 
person opportunities

00:19:36.846 --> 00:19:38.848
because I am in 
control in reverse.

00:19:38.848 --> 00:19:40.558
If I think that they're
being very appreciative

00:19:40.558 --> 00:19:42.039
or they see the big picture.

00:19:42.039 --> 00:19:43.948
So one of the things that
I think is quite important

00:19:43.948 --> 00:19:47.348
is not manipulating one's truth.

00:19:47.348 --> 00:19:49.996
And so, to me, somebody
taking advantage of me

00:19:49.996 --> 00:19:51.462
is very common.

00:19:51.462 --> 00:19:54.975
A, I'm most comfortable in
being taken advantage of

00:19:54.975 --> 00:19:56.969
because I like the leverage of it.

